`stacksave.u32 %r1;` in place of the redux gives the same result.

A probe function that writes the area (for example lat's entry) where the guard of a ret sits on its own line:
	st.u32 	[%rd9], %rd14;
	@%p1
	ret;
	... (more code, possibly more stores to the area)
$L__BB0_2:
	ret;
plan_by_value accepts it. pieces() sees "@%p1\n\tret" as one guarded ret (rets=1 for it), and is_ret_line("\tret;") also counts 1, so ret_lines == rets. Only accesses are refused when guarded; rets are not.

findings.effect:
by_value_rewrite inserts tv_give_back between the guard line and "\tret;". The result is
	@%p1
	mov.b64 	%bptv_x, {%bptv_h0, %bptv_h1};
	st.param.b64 	[F_bpftime_tv_r0], %bptv_x;
	ret;
The guard now applies to the mov, and the st.param and the ret are unconditional. With %p1 false the function returns early, skipping the rest of the probe, and returns a stale %bptv_x: the input word, or with a 16-byte area the last word loaded in the prologue, now returned as word 0. The kernel stores that into __bpftime_tv. These are wrong values, and the control flow changes. In the memory form the guarded ret stays guarded.

findings.input:
plan_by_value counts accesses and rets with pieces() over the whole body, where comments are stripped and directives without ';' are glued on. It then checks the per-line counts (line_statement and is_ret_line on each line) only for equal totals, not for the same lines. One copy that only the line scan sees, plus one real statement that only pieces sees, therefore passes.
(a) Access:
	/*
	st.u32 	[%rd9], %rd14;
	*/
	mov.u64 	%rd20, 0; st.u32 	[%rd9], %rd14;
(b) Ret, in a writing function:
	.loc 1 5 3
	ret;
...
$L__BB0_4: ret;
(pieces does not count the glued ".loc ..\n\tret" as a ret but counts "$L__BB0_4: ret"; the line scan counts the reverse.)

findings.effect:
(a) The emitted text is
	@p add.s32 %r5, %r5, 1; @p call (%bpftime_tv_r0), __retprobe_func__K, (%bpftime_tv_a0);
	@p add.s32 %r5, %r5, 1; @p st.local.u64 	[__bpftime_tv+0], %bpftime_tv_r0;
The add runs twice when p is set, so %r5 gets a wrong value. In the memory form the predicate text is emitted once.
(b) line.substr(semi) keeps "; @%p3 bra $L__BB0_4;" on the call line, and the st.local write-back comes on the next line. When %p3 is set the branch skips it, and the probe's write to the area is lost, so a later probe reads a stale value. In the memory form the probe wrote memory directly.

findings.input:
Entry probe (key 0 of thread-local map 4, as lat):
  {callseq ... _bpf_helper_ext_0001 ... ld.param.b64 %rd9, [retval0+0];}
  setp.eq.s64 %p1, %rd9, 0;
  @%p1 bra $L__BB0_2;
